From d652ec53f6034d7c138df274302df7dac94cb815 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 15:06:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 115/206] feat(shorebird): land analyze_snapshot --disassemble

The last unimplemented flag in analyze_snapshot's Shorebird group. Our --help is
now their --help byte for byte, with nothing withheld.

It was skipped for several rounds on the grounds that the name collides with the
VM's own --disassemble, and a source comment claimed an earlier attempt had been
reverted for that reason. No such revert exists in this history, and the
collision turns out to be the behaviour rather than an obstacle to it:

    theirs --disassemble           -> --shorebird JSON grows 698828 -> 1894974
    theirs --disassemble=true      -> 698828 bytes, no effect
    theirs --no_disassemble        -> 698828 bytes, no effect
    theirs --bogus_flag            -> Unrecognized flags: ...

A VM bool flag accepts the "=true" spelling; dart_bin's DEFINE_BOOL_OPTION
matches only the bare token. The bare token acting while "=true" does nothing is
what proves their own option parser consumes it and the VM never sees it. So
theirs does take the bare spelling away from the VM, and reproducing them means
doing the same. Nothing is lost in practice: analyze_snapshot loads an AOT
snapshot and compiles nothing, so FLAG_disassemble has no code to act on here,
and "=true" still reaches the VM.

The output shape is measured on their base.so, not chosen:

  * a "disassembly" key on every one of their 1380 entries, positioned between
    "size" and the hashes;
  * an array of human-readable instruction strings, no addresses and no hex;
  * one string per instruction, and instruction_count * 4 == size for all 1380;
  * branch targets spelled relative ("bl +100"). That is
    FLAG_disassemble_relative -- disassembler_arm64.cc:423 switches to "%+" Pd64
    under it and prints an address otherwise -- and their output contains zero
    branches rendered as an 0x address. The flag is set for the dump and
    restored, since the rest of the VM reads it.
  * shorebird mode only: their plain JSON is 878599 bytes with the flag and
    without, byte identical. So the flag is a parameter on the shorebird entry
    point rather than plumbed globally.

Verified by disassemble_check.sh in the research tree, which asserts the
contract on their binary first and then on ours, and additionally decodes
nop/brk/b/bl straight out of our .so through the ELF program headers: 3817 of
3817 agree, including the bl offsets computed from imm26, which is the
arithmetic that separates relative spelling from absolute.

Negative control: flipping FLAG_disassemble_relative to false rebuilds clean
(ninja exit 0) and turns that check red on exactly the two assertions that
should notice -- 0 relative / 21566 absolute, and the independent decode falling
to 1670/3817. The help check stays green under that same break, which is why the
behavioural check had to exist alongside it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

